<main id="main">

<section class="mk-hero"><div class="mk-plaid mk-hero-p" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="mk-w mk-hero-g"><div class="mk-hero-t"><p class="mk-ey">Wool jackets &middot; Buffalo plaid &middot; Field care</p><h1>The <span>Mackinaw</span> Jacket</h1><p class="mk-lead">A guide to the short, heavy wool coat of the north woods: where it came from, how to choose a weight, how to read a plaid and how to keep one going for decades.</p><div class="mk-hero-a"><a class="mk-btn" href="#weight">Pick a wool weight</a><a class="mk-btn mk-btn-o" href="#plaid">Build a plaid</a></div></div>
<div class="mk-hero-v"><figure class="mk-frame"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1759262988407-63535cd8b3ef?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=640&q=75" alt="a man in sunglasses wearing a plaid jacket outdoors" width="520" height="640" fetchpriority="high"></figure><figure class="mk-tag-img"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1588795904317-2f4ab1a0a852?auto=format&fit=crop&w=260&h=260&q=75" alt="red and black checked wool textile" width="260" height="260" fetchpriority="high"></figure><div class="mk-label"><span>100% wool</span><b>24 oz</b><span>Made to last</span></div></div></div></section>

<section class="mk-tr"><div class="mk-w mk-tr-g"><div class="mk-tr-h"><p class="mk-ey">What makes a mackinaw</p><h2 class="rv">Four things to look for</h2></div><ol class="mk-traits stg"><li class="rv"><span>1</span><b>Short cut</b><p>Ends at the hip, so it does not tangle in brush or snow.</p></li><li class="rv"><span>2</span><b>Heavy wool</b><p>Usually 24 to 30 oz per yard, dense and wind-resistant.</p></li><li class="rv"><span>3</span><b>Big pockets</b><p>Four or more, often with flaps, for gloves and tools.</p></li><li class="rv"><span>4</span><b>Double front</b><p>Some have a doubled yoke across the shoulders for rain.</p></li></ol></div></section>

<section class="mk-s mk-wt" id="weight"><div class="mk-w mk-wt-g"><div><p class="mk-ey">Tool one</p><h2 class="rv">Wool weight picker</h2><p class="mk-p">Wool cloth is sold by weight in ounces per yard. Heavier cloth is warmer and blocks more wind, but it is stiffer and hotter indoors. Choose a weight to see where it fits.</p>
<div class="mk-wsel" id="wt-s" role="group" aria-label="Wool weight"><button type="button" data-v="12">12<small>oz</small></button><button type="button" data-v="16">16<small>oz</small></button><button type="button" data-v="20">20<small>oz</small></button><button type="button" data-v="24" class="on">24<small>oz</small></button><button type="button" data-v="26">26<small>oz</small></button><button type="button" data-v="30">30<small>oz</small></button></div><div class="mk-wt-r" aria-live="polite"><h3 id="wt-t"></h3><p id="wt-d"></p><dl><div><dt>Comfortable from</dt><dd id="wt-c"></dd></div><div><dt>Best for</dt><dd id="wt-a"></dd></div><div><dt>Layer under</dt><dd id="wt-l"></dd></div></dl></div></div>
<div class="mk-therm"><div class="mk-th-in"><div class="mk-th-bar"><i id="wt-f"></i></div><ul class="mk-th-k" aria-hidden="true"><li>50&deg;F</li><li>35&deg;F</li><li>20&deg;F</li><li>5&deg;F</li></ul></div><div class="mk-cloth mk-plaid" id="wt-cl" aria-hidden="true"></div><p class="mk-th-n">Cloth thickness, to scale</p></div></div></section>

<section class="mk-s mk-pl" id="plaid"><div class="mk-w mk-pl-g"><div class="mk-pl-sw"><div class="mk-swatch" id="pl-v" role="img" aria-label="Plaid preview"></div><p class="mk-pl-cap" id="pl-n">Classic buffalo check</p></div>
<div><p class="mk-ey">Tool two</p><h2 class="rv">Plaid pattern builder</h2><p class="mk-p">A buffalo check is just two colours woven over and under each other in equal blocks. Pick a ground, a check colour and a size, and watch the cloth weave itself.</p>
<p class="mk-lb">Ground colour</p><div class="mk-sws" id="pl-a"><button type="button" data-c="#B22222" style="--c:#B22222" class="on" aria-label="Buffalo red"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#1A1A1A" style="--c:#1A1A1A" aria-label="Black"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#2E4A2E" style="--c:#2E4A2E" aria-label="Forest"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#1F3A5F" style="--c:#1F3A5F" aria-label="Navy"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#C9A227" style="--c:#C9A227" aria-label="Mustard"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#F3EAD8" style="--c:#F3EAD8" aria-label="Wool cream"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#6B4A2E" style="--c:#6B4A2E" aria-label="Bark"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#5A6B7A" style="--c:#5A6B7A" aria-label="Slate"><span></span></button></div><p class="mk-lb">Check colour</p><div class="mk-sws" id="pl-b"><button type="button" data-c="#B22222" style="--c:#B22222" aria-label="Buffalo red"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#1A1A1A" style="--c:#1A1A1A" class="on" aria-label="Black"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#2E4A2E" style="--c:#2E4A2E" aria-label="Forest"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#1F3A5F" style="--c:#1F3A5F" aria-label="Navy"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#C9A227" style="--c:#C9A227" aria-label="Mustard"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#F3EAD8" style="--c:#F3EAD8" aria-label="Wool cream"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#6B4A2E" style="--c:#6B4A2E" aria-label="Bark"><span></span></button><button type="button" data-c="#5A6B7A" style="--c:#5A6B7A" aria-label="Slate"><span></span></button></div>
<label class="mk-lb" for="pl-z">Check size: <b id="pl-zv">2 in</b></label><input type="range" id="pl-z" min="1" max="4" step=".5" value="2" class="mk-rng">
<label class="mk-ck"><input type="checkbox" id="pl-o"> Add an overcheck stripe</label></div></div></section>

<section class="mk-s mk-hs"><div class="mk-w"><div class="mk-hs-h"><p class="mk-ey">Two centuries in wool</p><h2 class="rv">From fort to fireside</h2></div><div class="mk-hs-g"><ol class="mk-tl"><li class="rv"><b>1811</b><div><h3>A cold winter at Fort Mackinac</h3><p>Soldiers on Lake Huron needed coats; local wool blankets were cut and sewn into short, warm jackets.</p></div></li><li class="rv"><b>1850s</b><div><h3>Lumber camp uniform</h3><p>Loggers took to the thick, short coat because it stayed warm when damp and did not catch on brush.</p></div></li><li class="rv"><b>1900s</b><div><h3>Buffalo plaid</h3><p>The bold two-colour check became the look of the north woods, worn by hunters and trappers.</p></div></li><li class="rv"><b>Today</b><div><h3>Weekend wardrobe</h3><p>The same cut and cloth now turns up on city streets as much as at the campfire.</p></div></li></ol><div class="mk-hs-i"><figure class="rv"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1772955543023-a6e99819e161?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=300&q=75" alt="a log cabin in a foggy forest" width="420" height="300" loading="lazy"></figure><figure class="rv"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1565718623908-2e1bf12a4aa1?auto=format&fit=crop&w=300&h=300&q=75" alt="an axe resting on a log" width="300" height="300" loading="lazy"></figure><figure class="rv"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1782227282777-31e421d02eaf?auto=format&fit=crop&w=300&h=300&q=75" alt="warm flannel shirts hanging on a rail" width="300" height="300" loading="lazy"></figure></div></div></div></section>

<section class="mk-camp"><div class="mk-camp-bg"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1758272960374-343f5eb7fdcd?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&h=800&q=75" alt="friends around a campfire by a lake at dusk" width="1600" height="800" loading="lazy"></div><div class="mk-w mk-camp-in"><p class="mk-ey">Field guides</p><h2 class="rv">Read by the fire</h2><div class="mk-gd-g"><a class="mk-gc rv" href="what-is-a-mackinaw-jacket-guide.html"><span class="mk-gc-n">No. 1</span><b>What Is a Mackinaw Jacket?</b><p>History, cut, cloth and how it differs from a shirt jacket.</p><i aria-hidden="true">Read the guide &rarr;</i></a><a class="mk-gc rv" href="caring-for-wool-jackets-guide.html"><span class="mk-gc-n">No. 2</span><b>Caring for Wool Jackets</b><p>Brushing, airing, spot cleaning, storing and mending.</p><i aria-hidden="true">Read the guide &rarr;</i></a></div></div></section>

</main>
